      "writtenOn": "2026-10-28T03:14:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It looks like this captures the offset that `vma_folio_offset(vma, address)` already gives: since aosp/4366901, aosp/4368054 and aosp/4368056, 4KB PTEs are built with `vma_pte_add_slice()`, so each maps the slice that `vma_slice_offset()` returns for its address. `get_dump_page()` (aosp/4312509), the iov_iter and process_vm_access helpers (aosp/4312497, aosp/4312492) and the futex key (aosp/4368034) all rely on that.\n\nAs far as I can see, only the driver-selected slices of `vm_insert_page_slice()` (aosp/4367599, the `{ 3, 1, 0, 2 }` fixture in aosp/4367986) break it, and supporting them would mean converting all of those users, not just GUP.\n\nIf those insertions can follow `vma_slice_offset()` instead, would it be OK to drop the `phys_offset` plumbing through the five `follow_*()` functions and fill the spans with `vma_folio_offset()` under `mmap_lock` in `gup_user_range()`, as `get_dump_page()` does? If they have to stay, could this `#ifdef` block become a helper in a P3S header, leaving a one-line call in `follow_page_pte()`?",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "8d636ac624a6a7addcd16547a830d98e005e9f85",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9a2da2bd_adc27ef1",
